 that we have here that means the  cases aren't as bad as in europe  or perhaps it's the climate the air in  the scene  foreign i have no idea  there must be some other reason  a few months ago they never thought  their lives would improve again so  quickly  but none of them will forget the anxious  period around guillermo's birth  anytime soon let's get more on the  uruguayan way and whether it can serve  as a model for others with marika  blowfield she's the director of the  latin  america division at the german institute  for global and area studies  she joins me by a skype from hamburg  welcome to dw america  latin america and the caribbean have  been heavily hit by the pandemic and its  economic ramifications  what really has uruguay done better so  far to cope with the crisis  well uruguay had a better health care  and social infrastructure going  into the pandemic and so when the  pandemic hit the government was well  prepared to mobilize its  state infrastructure to help with both  the health response  and make sure that populations were able  to stay inside shelter a home  and still feed their families and the  current government is also  following uruguayan's uruguay's very  institutionalized tradition a unifier  has been  unifying rather than  divisive and the population trusts the  government so they did as the government  said  uh and finally uruguay also had a  pioneering  veering via urologist who  was able to with colleagues invent his  own  test and that helped with massive rapid  testing  right from the beginning and so i think  those three factors have been very  important in helping uruguay  whether the pandemic much better than  any other latin american country  so in your opinion what is the takeaway  here for other countries  particularly those that might have less  money to spare for a solid  health care system there is  no better prevention than being well  prepared also from a social  social infrastructure point of view i  mean governments 